In C programming, you can terminate a program in several ways. The most common method is to use the `exit()` function from the `<stdlib.h>` library. This function allows you to terminate the program immediately and return an exit status to the operating system.

Here's an example of how to use the `exit()` function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Program is about to terminate.\n");
    exit(0); // Terminate the program with exit code 0, which typically indicates success.
    // The following line will not be executed because of the exit() call.
    printf("This line will not print.\n");
    return 0; // This line will also not be executed due to the exit() call.
}
```

